Problem Statement — Blinkit Cross-Category Discovery

Project: Graduation Project — Growth PM Case Study
Company: Blinkit (quick commerce)
Role: Product Manager, Growth Team
Strategic goal: Increase the % of Monthly Active Customers (MAC) who purchase from at least one new category every month.


1. Context: the business situation

Blinkit has won the hardest part of the quick-commerce game — habit formation. Users have folded the app into their weekly routine for groceries, snacks & beverages, and household essentials. Retention and order frequency in these core categories are healthy.

That same habit is now the constraint. Habit is efficient by design: it removes deliberation. A user who has learned "Blinkit = milk, bread, snacks, in 10 minutes" executes that routine without ever re-evaluating what else the platform sells. The platform has, in effect, trained its own users into a narrow lane.

This matters because Blinkit's assortment has expanded far beyond groceries — electronics, personal care & beauty, pharmacy, baby, pet, home & cleaning, intimate care — but usage has not expanded with it. The strategic goal is therefore not an assortment problem or an awareness campaign; it is a behaviour-change problem on an audience that is already active, already retained, and already transacting.


2. Evidence of the problem

2.1 Quantitative signal — what 32,999 public conversations show

Corpus analysed: 32,999 user-generated items across Play Store, App Store, Reddit (posts + comments), YouTube, HackerNews and the PissedConsumer complaint forum, spanning Jan 2023 – Sep 2025.

Finding 1 — Conversation is overwhelmingly concentrated in grocery.

