The existence of a stable, well-functioning set of workarounds is the clearest proof the need is real — and the clearest warning that a weak solution will simply be ignored.


6. Why solving this creates user value


Removes decision risk. Peer evidence at the point of decision replaces guesswork on an unfamiliar category.
Collapses effort. Consolidating a purchase currently spread across 3–4 apps into one 10-minute delivery is a real convenience gain, not a nudge.
Makes trial economically rational. If the fee overhead on a small first basket is neutralised, the cost of being wrong falls to near zero.
Serves genuine unmet demand. Respondents volunteered categories they want on Blinkit — a wider footwear range, a better book selection — and said they would switch if range improved. Demand exists; the bridge does not.


7. Why solving this makes business sense


Margin mix. Grocery is a thin-margin, high-frequency workhorse. Non-grocery categories (personal care & beauty, electronics accessories, pet, baby) carry materially higher margins. Moving basket mix, not just basket count, is how quick-commerce unit economics improve. (Exact margin deltas to be validated against internal finance data.)
Retention and defensibility. A user transacting in 3 categories has 3 reasons to open the app instead of 1. Multi-category users are structurally harder for a competitor to take — and the interview data shows single-category users are already being taken.
Assortment ROI. Blinkit has already paid the cost of stocking these categories in dark stores. Inventory that 92% of users never see is capital tied up without return. This is a demand-side unlock on supply that already exists.
Compounding data asset. Every cross-category trial generates preference data that improves subsequent recommendations for every user — a flywheel a competitor cannot copy by shipping the same feature, because they would not have the trial data underneath it.



8. Success metrics

North star metric

Cross-Category Activation Rate (CCAR) — % of MAC who purchase from at least one new category in a given month.


Numerator: count of monthly active customers who, in month M, place ≥1 order containing ≥1 item from an L2 category from which they have made no purchase in the preceding 90 days
Denominator: total monthly active customers in month M (≥1 order placed)
Grain: L2 category; 90-day lookback so a lapsed-and-returned category still counts as re-activation, while a monthly-repeating category does not inflate the metric
Why this metric: it is the direct operationalisation of the strategic goal, it is behavioural rather than attitudinal (a purchase, not a click), and the 90-day window prevents the metric being gamed by users cycling between two habitual categories.


Supporting metrics

MetricDefinitionWhy it is trackedNew-category trial rateUsers with ≥1 new-category item added to cart / MACIsolates intent from conversion — separates "wouldn't consider" from "considered and dropped off"Second-purchase rate (retention of trial)Users who repeat-purchase in a new category within 30 days of first trial / users who made a first trialThe real test. First-experience determinism means a trial that does not repeat has likely burned the category permanentlyTrial basket AOV & fee ratioFees as % of trial-order valueDirectly measures the economic barrier identified in Pattern CCategory spread per userDistinct L2 categories purchased per user per monthTracks depth of the behaviour change, not just the first conversion

Counter-metrics (guardrails)


Core-order friction: grocery order completion rate and time-to-checkout must not degrade. The primary habit loop is the business; protecting it outranks the growth goal.
Category-abandonment rate: users who trial and then never return to that category. A rising number here means the intervention is driving trials into a bad experience — which is worse than no intervention, because it burns categories at scale.
Return/complaint rate on newly-trialled categories, watched closely for the categories that already fail disproportionately.


Baseline: to be established from internal transaction data. (Public review data cannot measure purchase behaviour — corpus category share, where non-grocery is 8.5% of conversation, is a directional proxy only, not a baseline.)


9. Scope

In scope


AI-powered discovery engine over 32,999 public items (Play Store, App Store, Reddit, YouTube, HackerNews, PissedConsumer), Jan 2023 – Sep 2025
Primary research: 7 in-depth interviews with the target segment
A deployed AI-native MVP addressing the root cause for habituated grocery regulars
Metrics framework, failure modes and mitigation, competitive moat, iteration roadmap


Out of scope


Fee restructuring and pricing policy (finance-owned; identified as a contributing force, flagged as a dependency, not solved here)
Dark-store assortment expansion and supply-chain changes
Quality assurance and returns process re-engineering (a genuine root-cause contributor, but a separate operational programme — the MVP must instead be designed so it does not push users toward categories likely to fail them)
New user acquisition; loyalty and subscription redesign


Key assumptions to be validated


Non-grocery margins are materially higher than grocery (needs internal finance validation)
Peer evidence surfaced in-app is accepted as a substitute for a friend's recommendation — respondents rejected generic platform suggestions, so the MVP must be designed and tested against this specific risk
Sample of 7 in one tier-2 city is directionally representative; it should be pressure-tested against a broader quantitative survey before large-scale rollout



10. Problem statement in one line

Habituated Blinkit grocery regulars will not try a new category because trying is a high-risk, low-information, fee-penalised bet whose downside is permanent — and Blinkit currently supplies no in-app peer evidence to de-risk that first trial, so users route those purchases to specialist platforms instead.
